-- title:
--   Finite generation of an eigenform's coefficient ring
-- statement:
--   Let $M\ge 1$ be a natural number, let $\varepsilon$ be a Dirichlet character modulo $M$ with values in $\mathbb C$, and let $h$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ for $\Gamma_1(M)$. Write $a_n =$ [`ModularFormClass.qCoeff h n`](def/FLTPrelim_Modularity.html#L19) for the $n$-th coefficient of the $q$-expansion of $h$ of width $1$. Assume [`CuspForm.IsEigenformWith ε h`](def/CuspForm_PrimitiveFormGamma1.html#L19), i.e. the four conditions: $a_1 = 1$; for every prime $p$ with $p \nmid M$ and every $n \in \mathbb N$, $a_{pn} + \varepsilon(p)\,p\,\delta_{p\mid n}\,a_{n/p} = a_p a_n$ (the correction term being $0$ unless $p \mid n$, and the power of $p$ being $p^{k-1}$ with $k = 2$); for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \mid M$ and every $n$, $a_{\ell n} = a_\ell a_n$; and the nebentypus relation $h(\gamma\tau) = \varepsilon(d)\,(c\tau + d)^2 h(\tau)$ for all $\tau$ in the upper half-plane and all $\gamma \in SL_2(\mathbb Z)$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M)$, where $c = \gamma_{10}$ and $d = \gamma_{11}$ and $\varepsilon$ is evaluated at $d \bmod M$. The conclusion is that the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathbb C$ generated by the set of all coefficients $a_n$, $n \in \mathbb N$, together with all values $\varepsilon(u)$ for $u \in (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$, is, as a $\mathbb Z$-submodule of $\mathbb C$, finitely generated.
--
--   This is the weight-two, nebentypus form of Shimura's integrality theorem for Hecke eigenvalues: the ring generated by the Fourier coefficients of a normalised eigenform and the values of its character is a finite $\mathbb Z$-module, so all $a_n$ are algebraic integers lying in a number field. It feeds the construction of the finite Hecke algebra attached to such an eigenform and, through that, the Galois representations and Tate-module arguments used further on.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CuspForm_IsEigenformWith_fg_adjoin_qCoeff.lean

